+## What it does
+
+`/payoff` answers one question about a batch of tickets: what does finishing them actually get you? It reads every ticket in the batch in full and the code the batch touches, then explains the **delta** — the difference between the codebase today and the codebase when the last ticket closes — so a human understands it.
+
+Every claim it makes is **traceable**: each benefit names the ticket that delivers it, and a benefit with no ticket behind it is cut as filler. The whole skill is read-only — the answer is the artifact, and nothing is written to the tracker, the code, or the docs.
+
+## When to reach for it
+
+You invoke this by typing `/payoff` — the agent won't reach for it on its own.
+
+Reach for it whenever a batch of tickets exists and someone asks what it buys. That someone may be you before starting ("is this batch worth running?"), mid-flight ("what's left to gain?"), or a stakeholder you need to brief. The batch arrives in whatever form you hold it:
+
+- Ticket ids, numbers, or URLs listed explicitly
+- A parent issue, milestone, or wayfinder map — its still-open children
+- A feature slug on a local tracker (`.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/`)
+- Nothing but "this batch" — it asks which slice of the tracker you mean
+
+For building the work rather than explaining it, use [`/implement`](https://aihero.dev/skills-implement). For judging incoming issues one at a time, use [`/triage`](https://aihero.dev/skills-triage).
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+The issue tracker must have been configured by [`/setup-matt-pocock-skills`](https://aihero.dev/skills-setup-matt-pocock-skills), so `/payoff` knows where the batch lives — GitHub, GitLab, or local markdown.
+
+## Delta, not description
+
+The cheap answer to "what's the payoff?" is a paraphrase of the ticket titles. The skill refuses that: a delta needs its **before**. For each area the batch touches it reads the current code — through the project's domain glossary and ADRs — until it can state what hurts today, what works today, and what each ticket changes. The payoff is that difference, stated as before → after, so you can check it against the code you already know.
+
+A finished tracer bullet is demoable, so user-facing deltas name the behaviour that works end-to-end; codebase deltas name the pain that disappears. What the batch deliberately leaves alone is stated too — an out-of-scope line is what keeps the verdict honest.
+
+## Traceable or cut
+
+The one rule that shapes every answer: **traceable or cut**. Generic benefits — "improved maintainability", "better developer experience" — with no ticket behind them are filler, and filler is cut. On a wayfinder map the verdict starts from the map's stated **Destination**, then earns it from the tickets. Tickets already landed get credit as landed, so a mid-flight batch reports what's gained and what remains. And the answer comes back in the language the question was asked in.
+
+## Common questions
+
+**Does it count tickets that already landed?**
+
+Yes. Every ticket in the batch is read in full regardless of state; landed ones are marked as landed and credited, so the summary reflects the batch's real current position rather than pretending nothing has happened yet.
+
+**Does it write anything anywhere?**
+
+No. It touches nothing — no tracker comments, no commits, no doc updates. The summary is the artifact; paste it wherever you need it.
+
+## It's working if
+
+- Every benefit names the ticket that delivers it — no orphan adjectives floating free of a ticket.
+- Each "what changes" line states before → after, and the before matches the code you know.
+- Landed tickets are marked as landed; the summary reflects the batch's real current state.
+- The verdict is a few plain sentences you could read aloud to a stakeholder, in the language you asked in.
+- What the batch deliberately doesn't do is stated, not implied.
+
+## Where it fits
+
+A reach-for-it-anytime standalone that hangs off the ticket chain (`/grill-with-docs` → `/to-spec` → `/to-tickets` → `/implement` → `/code-review`): any time after [`/to-tickets`](https://aihero.dev/skills-to-tickets) has published a batch — or a [`/wayfinder`](https://aihero.dev/skills-wayfinder) map has children — and someone asks what finishing it buys. `/to-tickets` produced the batch it reads; `/implement` builds the work it explains.
+
+For the full map of how the skills fit together, see [ask-matt](https://aihero.dev/skills-ask-matt).
